Duration: 2 hours 8 minutes
Peak Viewers: 324
Average Viewers: 198
New Followers: 70
Total Followers: 218
Donations: $177.00
Subscribers: 9

$177. In a morning stream. The number on the dashboard is larger than my weekly rent payment used to seem.

I close OBS. Close VoxMorph. Take off the wig. The face in the dead webcam preview returns   Eli, pale, tired, the lashes and brows looking orphaned without their frame. The person the 324 viewers don't know exists.

Marc is coming at 2. I have two hours.

I eat a microwave burrito. Rotate at one minute. But this time, while the microwave hums, I open the RegenaForm app on my phone. I don't select anything. I just... browse. The Tier 1 menu. The options I haven't purchased yet. Nose   500 $. Lips   400 $. Jawline   600 $. Cheekbones   400 $. The crop line, dropping lower with each purchase. The face reveal, getting closer with each modification.

I close the app. Eat the burrito. It tastes the same as every burrito   warm salt and texture   but today it tastes like fuel rather than defeat.

[[Marc's Visit|day_4_12_kira]]

:: day_4_12_kira
<<set $day_step = 12>>
<<set $hint = "Hangout with Marc">>
Marc arrives at 2:07, because Marc has never been on time for anything in his life and the seven-minute margin is actually an improvement over his usual fifteen.

The knock is his   two quick raps followed by a pause followed by one more, a rhythm he's been using since high school that he swears is from a song but can never remember which one. I open the door.

He's standing in the hallway in the oversized charcoal hoodie and basketball shorts   in January, as always, because Marcus exists in a personal climate zone that the rest of the planet hasn't agreed to   holding a Tupperware container of leftover curry in one hand and his phone in the other.

<<marc "Yo.">>

<<eli "Hey.">>

He walks in. Steps over the Monster can I still haven't picked up from two days ago. Scans the apartment the way he always does   the quick, unconscious welfare assessment, the brown eyes cataloging the state of things. Mattress: unmade but not disaster-level. Desk: monitors off, nothing alarming visible. Kitchen: burrito wrapper (only one today). Closet: door closed.

The closet door is closed. I closed it specifically before he arrived. The wig is inside, on its care form, buried beneath the clothing pile. The RegenaForm bag is behind the mini-fridge. The VoxMorph is minimized on my desktop but the monitors are off. The evidence is hidden. The apartment presents as the same shitty apartment Marc has been visiting for two years.

He drops the curry on the counter. Turns to me.

<<marc "You look...">>

My stomach drops. The lashes. The brows. He's going to see them. He looked at my face yesterday on the street and clocked the washed hair and nothing else, but that was thirty seconds in passing. Now he's in my apartment, under the overhead light (flicker, flicker, on), three feet away, doing the full Marcus scan 

<<marc "...rested. You actually sleeping?">>

<<eli "Yeah. Been sleeping better.">>

<<marc "Huh. The Kira effect.">>

<<eli "What?">>

<<marc "Getting some action fixes your circadian rhythm. It's science.">>

<<eli "I'm not getting action.">>

<<marc "You kissed a girl in a car two days ago. That's action.">>

<<eli "Kissing isn't action.">>

<<marc "For you it is. You peaked.">>

He grins. The easy Marcus grin, the one that communicates affection through insult. He opens the Tupperware   the curry is rice and chicken in a jarred sauce, Marcus's signature dish, the culinary equivalent of a B-minus but compared to my diet it's a Michelin star   and puts it on the hot plate.

<<marc "You eat today?">>

<<eli "I had a burrito.">>

<<marc "Before noon?">>

<<eli "...ten-ish.">>

<<marc "He's eating before noon AND sleeping. The transformation is real.">>

The word "transformation" lands in my chest like a stone thrown through a window. He means it casually   the transformation of my habits, my mood, the Kira effect   but the word has a second meaning that he doesn't know about, a meaning that's sitting on a care form in my closet and running as a software plugin on my desktop and reshaping the bone structure above my eyes right now, still, the brows still settling into their final arch seventy-two hours after Dr. Maren's cool gel.

<<eli "Yeah. Feeling better.">>

<<marc "Good. You look it.">>

He doesn't specify what "it" looks like. He doesn't say "your eyelashes are longer" or "your eyebrows are different" or "there's something about your face that's changed." He just says I look better and means it generally   less tired, less grey, less like a person watching themselves decay in real time. The specific changes   the lashes, the brows   are invisible to him. Absorbed into the general impression of "Eli's doing better." The modifications are too subtle for a person who isn't looking for them, and Marcus isn't looking because Marcus doesn't care what faces look like. He cares what people feel like. And I feel better. So I look better. QED.

The test passes again. Day two of walking around in a modified face and nobody in my real life has noticed.

We eat the curry standing at the counter because I don't have a dining table, or a dining anything. The curry is good   warm, slightly too salty, the jarred sauce doing most of the work while the rice does the rest. Marcus eats with the focused efficiency of a person who views food as fuel, same as me, except his fuel is home-cooked and mine comes from a freezer.

<<marc "So. Kira.">>

<<eli "What about Kira.">>

<<marc "You seeing her today?">>

<<eli "She's picking me up at 6. Thai food.">>

<<marc "Thai food. That's a step up from ramen.">>

<<eli "She's paying.">>

<<marc "She always pays.">>

<<eli "Marc ">>

<<marc "I'm not judging. She can afford it. I'm just saying   you should be able to treat her sometimes. That's boyfriend stuff.">>

Boyfriend. He said boyfriend. The word is   it's new. Kira and I haven't used it. We've been together for two days, which feels like either a week or a century depending on which metric you use, and the label hasn't been applied. But Marcus applies it the way Marcus applies everything   directly, without ceremony, as though the thing he's naming has been obvious to everyone except the person he's naming it to.

<<eli "I'm not her boyfriend.">>

<<marc "You kissed her. Twice. She drives you places. She buys you food. She texts you good morning with heart emojis. That's a boyfriend, Eli.">>

<<eli "We haven't talked about it.">>

<<marc "You don't have to talk about it for it to be real.">>

He rinses his Tupperware in the sink   an act of domestic competence that I watch with the admiration of someone who doesn't own dish soap. Then he heads to the couch. The mattress. He sits on the mattress because I don't have a couch, and the mattress groans beneath him.

<<marc "Rift Walkers?">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

I grab the controllers from the desk. Hand Marcus the good one   not the sticky one, because I only have one controller at home and it's the good one and Marcus brought it over months ago and forgot to take it back. I plug in the second one, the cheap replacement I bought at a thrift store for $8, and sit beside him on the mattress.

The PS4 (not a PS5, the PS4, the one Marcus gave me when he upgraded, the hand-me-down that represents approximately 40% of my net worth) boots up. Rift Walkers loads. The dungeon generates.

[[Play Rift Walkers|day_4_12.1_other]]

:: day_4_12.1_other

We play.

The rhythm is the same. The same yelling, the same panic, the same call-and-response that strips the layers of bullshit away and leaves two friends in a room killing digital monsters.

<<eli "LEFT SIDE, LEFT SIDE ">>

<<marc "I SEE HIM, I'm out of charges ">>

<<eli "USE THE SWORD">>

<<marc "The sword does twelve damage, bro. He has four hundred health.">>

<<eli "Use it REPEATEDLY">>

<<marc "That's not a strategy, that's optimism.">>

We die on Floor 16. Our personal best. The death is spectacular   a chain reaction that starts when I step on a trap and ends when the boss does a room-wide attack that kills us both in the same frame. Marcus stares at the death screen for three seconds.

<<marc "Floor 16.">>

<<eli "Floor 16.">>

<<marc "Tied the record.">>

<<eli "Tied it.">>

<<marc "Next time we break it.">>

<<eli "Next time.">>

We run it again. Die on Floor 12. Again. Floor 9. The game is a meditation, a shared focus that requires enough attention to prevent thinking about anything else. For two hours, I'm not Ellie. I'm not ElliePlayz. I'm not a person with a wig in the closet and a voice modulator on the desktop and $177 on a dashboard that belongs to someone who doesn't exist. I'm Eli. I'm Marcus's friend. I'm a person sitting on a mattress eating leftover curry and dying on Floor 12 of a roguelike and arguing about sword damage.

Marcus is the anchor. He's always been the anchor   the fixed point that doesn't move while everything else shifts. He doesn't know about ElliePlayz. He doesn't know about RegenaForm. He doesn't know that the face he's sitting beside has been modified, that the brows have been sculpted, that the lashes are engineered. He sees what he's always seen: his friend. Looking better. Sleeping more. The Kira effect.

Between runs, during loading screens, he talks.

<<marc "So ScreenFixx is probably letting someone go.">>

<<eli "Who?">>

<<marc "Probably Devon. He dropped another phone last week. In the sink this time.">>

<<eli "How do you drop a phone in the sink?">>

<<marc "He was washing his hands and the phone was in his shirt pocket and he leaned over the sink and ">>

<<eli "Gravity.">>

<<marc "Gravity. The customer's face was incredible. Like watching someone's soul leave their body in real time.">>

<<eli "At least it wasn't the toilet this time.">>

<<marc "The toilet was Tyler. Tyler got fired. Devon is the sink guy. Keep your ScreenFixx lore straight.">>

We laugh. Real laughs. Not performed, not processed, not amplified for camera. Just two people on a mattress, laughing about a man who dropped a phone in a sink. The sound of my real laugh   my actual voice, my actual frequency   fills the apartment and it sounds different than it used to. Lighter. Less strained. Still male, still Eli, but the undertone of desperation that used to sit beneath every sound I made is... quieter. Not gone. But quieter.

<<marc "You seem good.">>

He says it during a loading screen, not looking at me, the casual observation delivered to the TV.

<<eli "I am good.">>

<<marc "Like actually good. Not the 'it's fine' good.">>

<<eli "Yeah. Actually good.">>

<<marc "The Kira thing?">>

<<eli "Partly.">>

<<marc "What's the other part?">>

I look at the loading screen. The dungeon generating. The randomized corridors and rooms and monsters assembling themselves from code, a world being built in real time from a set of rules and a random seed.

<<eli "I think... I'm figuring some stuff out. Career stuff.">>

<<marc "Streaming stuff?">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

<<marc "Good stuff?">>

<<eli "I think so. It's early. But the numbers are... better.">>

<<marc "On EliPlays?">>

A beat. A half-second pause that Marcus might not notice and might notice because Marcus notices things when they matter even if he doesn't notice things when they don't.

<<eli "Just... streaming in general. Trying some new approaches.">>

<<marc "Cool. About time.">>

He doesn't push. Marcus has the gift. The question was asked, the answer was given   incomplete, evasive, wrapped in vagueness   and Marcus accepts it the way he accepts everything I offer: with trust. He trusts me. The way Kira trusts me. The way the 324 viewers trust Ellie. Trust flowing in from every direction, and me spending it in a closet.

The loading screen ends. The dungeon generates. We play. We die. We play again.

At 4:30, Marcus stands. Stretches. The spine-crack that sounds medically concerning.

<<marc "I gotta bounce. Meeting some guys from the shop for pickup at the gym.">>

<<eli "Basketball?">>

<<marc "Yeah. You should come sometime.">>

<<eli "I would literally die.">>

<<marc "You'd last at least ten minutes.">>

<<eli "Generous.">>

He picks up his hoodie from where he draped it over the desk chair. I hold my breath for a half-second   the hoodie was two feet from the monitors, two feet from the OBS icon on the desktop, two feet from the corner of the screen where VoxMorph's taskbar icon sits. But the monitors are off. Everything is invisible.

<<marc "Have fun with Kira tonight.">>

<<eli "I will.">>

<<marc "Wear the grey shirt.">>

<<eli "I'm already wearing the grey shirt.">>

<<marc "Wear it again. It's working.">>

<<eli "Clothing advice from a man in basketball shorts in January.">>

<<marc "These shorts are a lifestyle choice. Your shirt is a survival strategy.">>

He heads for the door. Stops. Turns. The look   the Marcus look, the direct one, the one that holds you.

<<marc "I'm glad you're figuring stuff out, Eli. Whatever it is. You deserve to figure stuff out.">>

<<eli "Thanks, Marc.">>

<<marc "And if you need to talk about whatever the 'new approaches' are   I'm here. No judgment.">>

<<eli "I know.">>

<<marc "Alright. Later.">>

<<eli "Later.">>

The door closes. His footsteps in the hallway. The stairwell door. Then silence.

I stand in the apartment. The curry smell lingers. The mattress has a Marcus-shaped depression. The controller he used is warm from his hands.

No judgment. He said no judgment. He offered, the way he always offers, the open door that I keep not walking through. He'd understand. If I told him   Marcus, I made a second account and I'm streaming as a girl and I went to a clinic that modified my face and I'm earning $177 a morning   he'd listen. He'd process. He'd probably say something like "that's wild" and then ask if I'd eaten today.

But I can't tell him. Not yet. Not when the money is still proving itself. Not when the face is still half-finished. Not when the story doesn't have an ending yet   just a beginning and a middle and a dashboard that keeps climbing.

I close the door. It's 4:45. Kira picks me up at 6.

An hour and fifteen minutes to shower, change, and stop being Ellie's creator long enough to be Eli's self. Kira's Eli. The version of me that sits still while she sleeps and sends red hearts without hesitation and doesn't have a wig in the closet.

That version. The real one. Or the other real one. I'm losing track of which is which.
<<set $hint = "Go Outside to meet Kira">>
[[Wait till Kira Comes|apartment_map]]



:: day_4_13_kira

She picks me up at 6:03. Three minutes late. Cherry blossom. Heater. The warm enclosure that has become, in four days, the most reliable geography of my happiness.

<<kira "Hi!">>

She says it the same way every time   bright, singular, loaded with a warmth that makes the word sound like it weighs more than two letters. I lean across the console and she meets me halfway and the kiss is quick and easy, a hello that's becoming a habit, her lips warm and slightly glossy from the chapstick-that's-actually-lip-gloss and mine dry and cold from the January walk to the curb.

<<eli "Hey.">>

<<kira "You smell like curry.">>

<<eli "Marc came over. He brought leftovers.">>

<<kira "Aw, Marc. How is he?">>

<<eli "He's Marc. Someone at his job dropped a phone in a sink.">>

<<kira "Was it the toilet guy?">>

<<eli "Different guy. Sink is a different guy. The toilet guy got fired.">>

<<kira "ScreenFixx lore is getting complicated.">>

<<eli "Marcus keeps a mental database. He's the historian.">>

She laughs. Pulls into traffic. Her hand finds mine across the console before we've gone two blocks   the interlacing of fingers that's already becoming automatic, the geometry of small and large and warm and cold that fits without effort. I feel the pixel-heart tattoo against my palm. My thumb traces the edge of it through the skin, the faintest ridge of ink.

[[Go Inside|day_4_14_kira]]

:: day_4_14_kira
<<set $day_step to 14>>
<<set $hint = "Check the Menu and Order Food">>
The Thai place is called Lotus Room. Cheap. Small. Twelve tables, half of them empty on a Thursday evening. Red and gold decor that's trying hard and mostly succeeding. The smell of lemongrass and chili and coconut hits us at the door and Kira makes a sound   a small "mmm" of anticipation that I file away in the archive alongside every other sound she makes, the encyclopedia of Kira growing by the hour.
<<set $locationName to "Lotus Room">>
<img src="images/locations/lotus_room.jpeg" alt="Lotus Room Interior" class="scene-image">
<<set $locationImage to "images/locations/lotus_room.jpeg">>
We sit at a table by the wall. The lighting is warm   red-tinted pendant lamps   and Kira glows in it. The black turtleneck again (or a similar one), the wire-frame glasses catching the red lamplight, the bob tucked behind one ear on the left side the way she does when she's relaxed. She's beautiful in this light. She's beautiful in every light but in red light she looks like a painting, warm tones on warm skin, the sharpness of her features softened into something that makes my chest ache.

<<kira "I'm getting the pad thai. You?">>

I scan the menu. The prices are reasonable   $12-16 for mains. I can afford this. I have $177 on the ElliePlayz dashboard from this morning alone, plus the $210 from yesterday, plus the miscellaneous EliPlays income that's too small to mention. I can afford a $14 green curry. I can afford it easily.

But I can't tell her that. The money doesn't exist in the Eli timeline. In the timeline Kira knows about   the one where I have $47 in the bank and my last stream earned $3.50   I can't afford anything. The dashboard that says $318 in two days belongs to someone she doesn't know about. Someone named Ellie.

<<eli "Green curry.">>

<<kira "Spice level?">>

<<eli "Medium?">>

<<kira "Coward. I'm going extra hot.">>

<<eli "Your funeral.">>

<<kira "My taste buds, my rules.">>

She orders. In her minimal Thai   two words, pointing at the menu, a smile at the server that probably earns better service than fluent Thai would. The food comes fast. The pad thai is piled high with bean sprouts and crushed peanuts and a lime wedge. My green curry is a pool of emerald coconut milk with vegetables and chicken, steam rising.

We eat. We talk. The conversation flows the way it always does   spiraling, touching, releasing   and I realize, sitting across from her in the red light, that this is what a date feels like. Not the coffee shop with Marcus buying. Not the ramen where Kira framed it as "celebrating." This is a date. She's my... whatever we are. Girlfriend. The word Marcus used. The word that fits even if we haven't said it.

<<kira "Can I ask you something?">>

<<eli "Always.">>

<<kira "Are you happy?">>

The question is simple and impossible. Am I happy. The word contains multitudes. Am I happy that Kira is sitting across from me looking like a painting? Yes. Am I happy that $318 appeared in my life in two days? Yes. Am I happy that I'm lying to the person asking the question? No. Am I happy? The answer is yes and no and both and neither and the fact that I have to calculate it means the answer is complicated in a way that "happy" isn't designed to be.

<<eli "Yeah. I think I am.">>

<<kira "You THINK you are?">>

<<eli "I'm not used to it. I'm still calibrating.">>

<<kira "Calibrating happiness. That's the most Eli answer possible.">>

<<eli "What's the most Kira answer?">>

<<kira "The most Kira answer is: same. I'm calibrating too.">>

She reaches across the table. Her hand   small, warm, the pixel-heart tattoo   finds mine beside the green curry. Our fingers interlace on the tablecloth. Her thumb traces circles on the back of my hand, the now-familiar gesture, the circuit she draws on every surface when she's feeling something she hasn't named.

<<kira "This is nice. Just this. Eating food and holding hands and not thinking about stream schedules.">>

<<eli "You think about stream schedules on dates?">>

<<kira "I think about stream schedules in the shower, Eli. I'm a content creator. My brain doesn't have an off switch.">>

<<eli "I know the feeling.">>

More than she knows. We finish dinner. She reaches for the check.

<<eli "I can ">>

<<kira "Eli.">>

<<eli "Let me at least ">>

<<kira "I've got it. Don't make it weird.">>

She pays. The card taps. The receipt prints. And I sit there with $318 on a dashboard she doesn't know about and I let her pay for my dinner and the splinter pushes a millimeter deeper. After the next modification, I think. After the nose. After the face is more complete. I'll tell her then. I'll show her the numbers and she'll understand and then I can pay for everything. I can pay for every dinner from now until we're old.

Soon. Just not yet.
<<set $hint = "Go Back to Kira's Apartment">>
[[Go Back to Kira's Apartment|neighbourhood]]

:: day_4_15_kira

Back at Kira's apartment. The Civic parks. We take the elevator. She unlocks 3C. Packet is on the couch, occupying the center cushion with the territorial confidence of a cat who has never been told no. He looks at me. I look at him. The détente continues.

<<kira "Tea?">>

<<eli "Sure.">>

She disappears into the kitchen. I sit on the couch   Packet grudgingly relocates to the armrest with the displeased fluidity of a liquid poured from one container into another   and I look around the apartment. The same curated space. The same intentional warmth. Kira's setup is visible across the room   the dual monitors dark, the ring light off, the keyboard's RGB underglow faintly pulsing in standby mode like a sleeping heartbeat. The chibi figurine on the shelf. The acoustic foam. Everything in its place.

She returns with two mugs. Green tea. She hands me one and sits beside me   close, closer than necessary, the couch offering plenty of space and Kira choosing to use none of it. Her thigh presses against mine. Her shoulder finds the hollow of my arm.

<<kira "What do you want to watch?">>

<<eli "You pick.">>

<<kira "Frieren? We left off at episode four.">>

<<eli "The one where you fell asleep.">>

<<kira "I did NOT fall  okay, I fell asleep. But only because you were so comfortable.">>

<<eli "I'm a mattress with a pulse.">>

<<kira "The most romantic pulse-mattress I've ever slept on.">>

She queues the episode. The TV lights up   painted backgrounds, soft music, the elf mage and her journey. Kira tucks her feet under her and leans into me and my arm goes around her shoulders the way it did two days ago, the position already memorized by my body, muscle memory forming for a gesture I've performed exactly twice.

We watch. She doesn't fall asleep this time   she's more awake, more present, commenting on the show, her voice soft and close. I can feel her breathing against my side, the steady rise and fall of her ribcage.

The episode reaches an emotional scene   the elf realizing she missed something important about a person she lost   and Kira shifts. She turns her face toward me. I look down. She's close   closer than the car, closer than the izakaya   her face tilted up, the glasses at an angle, the brown eyes looking directly into mine from four inches away.

She kisses me.

Not the hello kiss. Not the goodbye kiss. This is a different kiss   slower, deeper, her hand coming up to the back of my neck, fingers in the short hair at my nape, pulling me down toward her. Her lips part against mine and the kiss opens and I taste green tea and something underneath that's just her, the specific chemistry of Kira's mouth, and my brain stutters and restarts in a different mode.

<img src="images/scenes/day_4/day_4_2.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment" class="scene-image">

I kiss her back. My hand finds her waist   the side of her body, the curve between ribcage and hip, felt through the black turtleneck, small and warm and real. She makes a sound against my mouth. A small sound. Not a word   something pre-verbal, something the body produces when it stops consulting the brain. "Mmnh." A vibration against my lips.

The kiss deepens. Her hand tightens at my nape. My hand on her waist pulls her closer   not dramatically, not aggressively, just a half-inch of reduced distance that she fills by pressing her whole side against me. The couch cushions shift. Packet, sensing that his territory is being used for purposes he doesn't approve of, leaps from the armrest and stalks toward the bedroom with the disgusted dignity of a cat who has standards.

<img src="images/scenes/day_4/day_4_3.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment" class="scene-image">

When the kiss breaks   when we pull apart for air, both breathing slightly harder, faces flushed   she stays close. Her forehead against mine. The wire-frame glasses bumping my brow bone. Her eyes, from this distance, are blurred   too close to focus, a wash of brown and warmth.

<<kira "Hi.">>

<<eli "Hi.">>

<<kira "Your eyes look different up close.">>

The sentence lands like a cold stone in warm water. My body goes still   a micro-freeze that I force myself to override, to smooth over, to bury beneath the warm fog of the kiss and the closeness and her forehead on mine.

<<eli "Different how?">>

<<kira "I don't know. Bigger? Your lashes are really long. Have they always been this long?">>

She's looking. From four inches away, with her analytical eyes, the eyes that notice brand changes in energy drinks and remember 3 AM voices. She's looking at my lashes   the engineered lashes, the RegenaForm lashes, the lashes that are longer and darker and denser than anything that was there four days ago.

<<eli "I think it's just... the lighting in here. Your lamp does something.">>

<<kira "Hmm.">>

The "hmm" again. The same one from the car two days ago. The syllable that means I don't believe you but I'm choosing not to press. She files it. I can almost see the filing   the mental folder labeled "Things About Eli's Face" getting a new entry.

I redirect. The only move.

<<eli "You look different up close too.">>

<<kira "How?">>

<<eli "More freckle. You have a freckle near your left eyebrow.">>

<<kira "I've always had that!">>

<<eli "I've never been close enough to see it.">>

<<kira "You've been close enough for two days. You're just not observant.">>

<<eli "I'm observant about other things.">>

<<kira "Like what?">>

<<eli "Like the way you make a sound when you kiss me. A little 'mmnh.'">>

Her face goes red. The flush starts at the neck and climbs past her jaw to her cheeks and the tips of her ears, visible even in the warm lamp light.

<<kira "I do NOT make a sound.">>

<<eli "You absolutely do. It's ">>

<<kira "Eli.">>

<<eli "It's incredibly cute.">>

<<kira "STOP.">>

She shoves my shoulder. I catch her hand. She lets me. We're holding hands on the couch and her face is red and mine is probably red too and the TV is still playing the anime neither of us is watching.

<<kira "You can't just notice things like that and SAY them.">>

<<eli "Why not?">>

<<kira "Because I've been trying to be COOL about this and you keep making it impossible.">>

<<eli "You're the coolest person I know.">>

<<kira "I drooled on your shirt last time.">>

<<eli "Coolly.">>

She laughs. The real laugh. And then she kisses me again.

[[Continue Kissing|day_4_16_kira]]

:: day_4_16_kira
<<set $day_step to 16>>
<<set $hint = "Enjoy the Moment with Kira">>
The second round of kissing is longer. Less tentative. We shift on the couch until she's half-turned toward me, her legs across my lap, my arm around her back. The position is closer than anything we've done   her weight on me, her body against mine, the full surface area of contact that two people can achieve while technically sitting on a couch.

<img src="images/scenes/day_4/day_4_4.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment" class="scene-image">

She kisses my jaw. My neck. The spot below my ear. Her lips are warm and her breath is hot and each point of contact sends a signal through my nervous system that is rapidly becoming difficult to manage. I'm hyper-aware of every place her body touches mine   her thigh across my lap, her hip against my side, her chest pressed against my arm.

Her chest.

I feel it through the turtleneck and through my grey shirt   the soft, specific pressure of her breasts against my arm. Soft round press can't ignore. The shape of them felt through two layers of fabric, warm, the kind of pressure that registers in a part of the brain that doesn't process words. She probably doesn't notice the contact   it's incidental, a consequence of the position, the geometry of two bodies close together. But I notice it. I notice it the way a person standing in a river notices the current.

The kissing continues. Her hand is on my chest, fingers curled into the fabric of my shirt. My hand is on her back, the warmth of her spine through the turtleneck. We're breathing harder. The TV plays unnoticed. The apartment is warm and close and the only sounds are us   the small wet sounds of mouths meeting and separating, the fabric shifting, the breathing and then   slowly, inevitably, with the reliability of a physical law   I get hard.

It's not subtle. It's not a slight stirring that could be ignored or attributed to a fold in the joggers. It's a full, insistent, undeniable erection that happens because a beautiful girl is on my lap and kissing my neck and her breast is against my arm and my body has made a decision that my brain did not approve.

I feel it. She has to feel it   her thigh is across my lap, the contact is direct, the evidence is pressing against her through two layers of fabric and there is no amount of subtle repositioning that will hide what's happening in my joggers.

I freeze. Not completely   the kiss doesn't stop   but internally I lock up, the panic arriving late to a party the body started without permission. Do I acknowledge it? Shift? Pull back? The etiquette of involuntary erections during early-relationship couch makeouts is not covered in any guide I've ever read.

Kira doesn't freeze. She doesn't pull back. She doesn't say anything. What she does is   nothing. She continues kissing my jaw, her hand still on my chest, her thigh still across my lap, and she adjusts her position by approximately one inch in a direction that is not away from the situation. The adjustment is so small it might be unconscious. It might not be. She shifts and the pressure changes and I feel the contact through the fabric and my brain produces a sound that is fortunately not processed by VoxMorph because it's just me on a couch and not Ellie on a stream.

She knows. She has to know. And she's choosing not to make it a thing.

The kissing slows. Naturally, organically, the way a wave recedes   not because it's forced back but because it reaches its natural shore. She pulls back. Lays her head on my shoulder. My arm stays around her. Her legs stay across my lap. The erection persists, insistent and embarrassing, and she rests against me as though it isn't there, granting me the dignity of pretending physics isn't happening.

<<kira "This is nice.">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

<<kira "You seem happy today. Like really happy. Not calibrating-happy. Actually happy.">>

<<eli "I am happy.">>

<<kira "Why?">>

<<eli "I'm with you.">>

<img src="images/scenes/day_4/day_4_5.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment" class="scene-image">

It comes out simple and true and the simplicity of it surprises me because the real answer is more complicated   I'm happy because I earned $177 this morning and $210 yesterday and the numbers are climbing and the face is changing and the future is opening   but the simple answer is also true. I'm with her. And being with her produces a specific happiness that the dashboard can't replicate. The money makes me hopeful. Kira makes me happy. They're different things and right now, on this couch, with her weight against me and her warmth in my arm, the difference matters.

<<kira "I'm happy too. For the record.">>

She nestles closer. Her breathing slows. The anime plays.
<<set $hint = "Relax and Enjoy Kira's Company">>
[[Stay on the Couch|day_4_17_kira]]

:: day_4_17_kira

Twenty minutes later, Kira stands. Stretches. Her turtleneck rides up, showing that strip of stomach above the waistband   smooth, fair skin, the small architecture of her navel   and I look and look away and she catches me looking and does the teasing grin.

<<kira "I'm going to shower. You stay. I'll make us dinner after.">>

<<eli "You're making dinner?">>

<<kira "I can cook, Eli. Not everyone lives on microwave burritos.">>

<<eli "What are you making?">>

<<kira "Rice and egg. The Japanese way. It's comfort food.">>

She disappears into the bathroom. The shower starts   I can hear the water through the door, the different sound a good shower makes compared to my apartment's dispirited drizzle. This is a real showerhead. Real water pressure. Real hot water that arrives on demand instead of after an eight-minute geological negotiation.

I sit on the couch. Packet has returned, sensing the romantic energy has dissipated, and has reclaimed the opposite cushion. He stares at me. I stare at him. The erection has subsided, finally, the body accepting that the immediate stimulus has moved to another room, though the memory of the pressure   her thigh, her chest, the contact   lingers in my nervous system like the echo of a bell.

I check my phone. Nothing urgent. The ElliePlayz dashboard on my phone browser still shows the morning numbers: $177. I close it. Open Kira's chat instead.

The shower stops. Minutes pass. Sounds of moving around. Then the bathroom door opens.

Kira walks out in a bathrobe.

Not a towel   a short cotton bathrobe, white, belted loosely at the waist, ending mid-thigh. Her legs are bare below it   smooth, fair skin from mid-thigh to bare feet on the hardwood. Her hair is wet, the bob darkened and slightly longer when heavy with water, the bangs plastered to her forehead. She's not wearing her glasses   the wire frames left in the bathroom, her face naked without them, the eyes larger and softer and more vulnerable without the barrier. Steam follows her from the bathroom. She smells like body wash   something floral and clean, the personal scent of her skin amplified by hot water.

<img src="images/scenes/day_4/day_4_6.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment" class="scene-image">

She walks to the kitchen. Casually. Like this is normal. Like walking past her boyfriend of three days in a short bathrobe with wet hair and no glasses is an unremarkable Tuesday evening event.

<<kira "Rice'll be ready in twenty. You want soy sauce or ponzu?">>

My mouth is dry. My brain is offline. I'm sitting on a couch looking at Kira Nomura in a bathrobe and the signal traveling from my eyes to my speech center has encountered a critical error.

<<eli "Uh.">>

<<kira "Soy sauce or ponzu. It's not a hard question.">>

<<eli "Soy sauce.">>

<<kira "Good choice.">>

<img src="images/scenes/day_4/day_4_7.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment" class="scene-image">

She moves through the kitchen with the easy confidence of a person who is comfortable in her own space and her own body. She opens the rice cooker. Steam rises. She cracks eggs into a bowl and whisks with chopsticks. The bathrobe shifts as she moves   not revealing anything explicit, but the fabric drapes and moves and suggests, the outline of her body visible through the thin cotton, the line of her waist and the curve of her hip and the shape of her breasts under the loosely belted robe.

I'm hard again. Immediately. Completely. The same insistent, undeniable, physics-defying erection that will not be reasoned with. I'm sitting on her couch with a throw pillow in my lap   placed there with the casual desperation of a person pretending to be comfortable   and Kira is making rice in a bathrobe and I am going to die on this couch.

She turns. Holds out a bowl   rice topped with a raw egg, soy sauce drizzled in a spiral, sesame seeds, a strip of nori. Simple. Beautiful. The food of a person who knows how to feed another person.

<<kira "Tamago kake gohan. My grandma's version.">>

She sits beside me. Cross-legged on the couch, the robe riding up on her thighs, the hem barely covering what it needs to cover. She mixes her egg into the rice with chopsticks, the steam curling, and she eats with the same focused precision she brings to everything   neat, efficient, the chopsticks moving quickly.

<<eli "This is really good.">>

<<kira "It's rice and egg, Eli.">>

<<eli "It's the best rice and egg I've ever had.">>

<<kira "You've been eating burritos for eight months. A saltine cracker would be the best thing you've ever had.">>

<<eli "Don't disrespect the saltine. Or the tamago. Both are sacred.">>

She kicks me lightly with her bare foot. Her toes against my ankle. I feel the contact like a bolt and the throw pillow in my lap becomes more necessary.

We eat. She tells me about her stream today   good numbers, the Q&A format working, her viewers sending increasingly specific questions ("someone asked my blood type. My BLOOD TYPE, Eli. For compatibility purposes."). I listen and respond and the conversation is normal, easy, the surface calm of a person whose subsurface is a catastrophe of arousal and guilt and the specific cognitive dissonance of eating comfort food made by a girl he's lying to.

After dinner, she puts the bowls in the sink. Returns. Sits close. The bathrobe has loosened slightly   the neckline wider than it was, showing more of her collarbone, the upper curve of her chest visible where the robe doesn't quite close. She either doesn't notice or doesn't mind.

<img src="images/scenes/day_4/day_4_8.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment" class="scene-image">

I look. I try not to look. I look again. The skin of her upper chest is smooth and fair and slightly flushed from the hot shower and I'm going to need the throw pillow permanently surgically attached to my lap if this continues.

<<kira "I should probably get dressed before I freeze.">>

<<eli "You look  I mean. Yeah. It's cold.">>

She gives me the teasing grin. The one where the left side pulls up higher. She knows exactly what she's doing. She has to know. Kira doesn't do anything without intention.

She goes to the bedroom. Returns in sweats and an oversized T-shirt   the uniform of a person who has decided the seduction portion of the evening is complete and the comfort portion has begun. The glasses are back on. The hair is drying, the bob returning to its natural shape. She looks like Kira again   sharp, comfortable, the girl with the stream and the cat and the pixel-heart tattoo.

We kiss goodnight at the door. A long one. Her hands on my chest, mine on her waist, the contact that I'm learning the geography of   the specific width of her ribcage under my palms, the heat of her skin through the t-shirt, the way she rises slightly on her toes because I'm taller and she's not wearing the platform sneakers.

<<kira "Goodnight, disaster.">>

<<eli "Goodnight.">>

<<kira "Stream tomorrow?">>

<<eli "Always.">>

<<kira "Same. Noon. Watch if you want.">>

<<eli "I'll be there.">>

<<kira "❤️">>

She says it out loud. Not a text, not an emoji   the word "heart" replaced by the look in her eyes, the squeeze of her hands, the last press of her lips against mine. Then the door closes and I'm in the hallway and the cold returns.

I walk home. Fifteen minutes. January. The cold is clarifying   it strips the arousal back to baseline, the frigid air doing what the throw pillow couldn't. By the time I reach my building, my body has calmed but my brain hasn't. The images persist: the bathrobe, the wet hair, the collarbone, the shape of her breasts through the cotton, her thigh across my lap, the pressure, the "mmnh" against my lips.
